devCb = { f,2->(\f c,4. des,8 | es,4-.) des,8( c,8 bes,,4-.) aes,,4-. | f,4-.\sf r4 r2 | R1 | bes,2->( f,4. ges,8 | aes,4-.) ges,8( f,8 es,4-.) des,4-. | bes,,4-.\sf r4 r2 | R1 | f,2\p\< e,2 | es,2 d,2 | des,2 c,2 | c,1\ff | f,2 des,2 | bes,,2 c,2 | f,2-> r2 | d,2.\p\> r4\! | g,4\pp^\markup \italic "pizz." r4 r2 | fis,4 r4 g,4 r4 | es,4 r4 r2 | c,4 r4 bes,4 r4 | a,4 r4 r2 | d,4 r4 r2 | g,4 r4 r2 | d,4 r4 r2 | g,1\pp^\markup \italic "arco" | fis,2 g,2 | e,1 | c,2 b,,2 | a,,1 | d,1 | g,1\< | d,1 | d,2\f d,2 | g,,4-> r4 r2 | g,1\>~ | g,2. r4\! | }
% c053 (form; A&S Unit 18, PDF 291-292: in a series of 6/3 chords only the beginning and the end are points of articulation, and the end matters for its cadential
% function; Ex. 18-8a, 18-12, PDF 295, 298-299: the 6/3 descent with 7-6 suspensions leads to the cadence): the lament's 6/3 descent (107-109) arrives on V at 110 and the
% F-minor cadence 110-113 is the development's goal. It was f with no trumpets or tuba, so the development's loudest point was below the exposition (27, 91 ff) and the
% retransition (147 ff). Dvorak 9/iv's development peaks ff with the trumpets (177-191) before the recapitulation. 110-113 now ff, trumpets 1-3 and tuba join
% (Tpt 1 on Ob 1's E5-F5 then Fl 1's F5-E5-F5, Tpt 2 = Tbn 1 an octave up and Vln II, Tpt 3 = Hn 1, tuba = basses), timpani C and A flat at 111-112.
% c097 (texture; Adler ch. 11, PDF 44-47, Bartok Concerto for Orchestra i 313-386: the fugato passes from strings to the brass, 'f, ben marc.'; two horns in unison = one trombone): 103-104 Hn 3-4 (a2, with the violas) and Tbn 1 (with the cellos) take the B flat minor head instead of a single punctuation chord, so the second statement is louder and brighter than the first (99, strings alone).
% c098 (expression; A&S Unit 21, PDF 348 sec. 4: dissonant suspensions 'receive something of an accent and their resolutions are softer'; PDF 350 sec. 8: the cadential 4-3 over V is the
% suspension's most important use; Dvorak 9/iv 176, 182, 190: trumpets and trombones ff with the strings at the development's climaxes): 110-113 horns, trumpets, trombones and tuba ff (were f since c054),
% so the climax's 9-10 (111) and cadential 4-3 (112, Tpt 1 and Hn 2 F-E with the violins) are no longer a level below the exposition codetta (91) and the retransition (147), both ff in full brass.
% Answer to c054: the melody's top octave (Vln I, Fl 1, G6-A flat6-F6-E6-F6) lies more than an octave above the highest brass note (Tpt 1 F5), and Tpt 1 itself doubles the G5 octave's F5-E5-F5. Timpani stay f (c056).
% c098: 105 Hn 3-4 and Tbn 1, which double the B flat minor head since c097, end it with the strings' sf. 108 Vln I's restruck 7th C6 over D (the 7-6) takes a tenuto (PDF 347: restruck suspension; PDF 348 sec. 4).
devTptOne = { R1*11 | e''1\ff | f''1 | f''2~ f''4 e''4 | f''2-> r2 | R1*21 | }
devTptTwo = { R1*11 | c''1\ff | c''2 des''2 | des''2 c''2 | c''2-> r2 | R1*21 | }
% c060 (cyclic lens; Sebesky ch. 1, PDF 11: a colour saved for its entrance keeps its surprise; arrival.ily: 223 is the first sustained forte C-major chord in full brass):
% 110 and 112 (second half) Tpt 3 holds B flat 4 (was G4 = Hn 1), so the brass sound V7 of F, not a root-position C-major triad, and the 7th falls to A flat 4 at 111 and 113.
devTptThree = { R1*11 | bes'1\ff | aes'2 aes'2 | bes'1 | aes'2-> r2 | R1*21 | }
devTuba = { R1*11 | c,1\ff | f,2 des,2 | bes,,2 c,2 | f,2-> r2 | R1*21 | }
